"""        <h2>The Scene <span class="em">🎬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Mixed news is the hardest news: too much praise and the problem gets lost, too much problem and the parent stops hearing you.</p>

        <p>You teach Year 7 English, and tonight is parents' evening — the conveyor belt of ten-minute conversations that parents remember for years. Lena Novak is twelve: a genuinely gifted writer, kind in a quiet classroom-changing way, and — since half term — a girl who has stopped handing things in and gone silent in discussions she used to lead.</p>

        <p>Across the little table is her mother, who has come straight from work and wants, like every parent on the list, to hear that everything is fine. Your job is precision under time pressure: real praise that isn't packaging, one clearly named problem with evidence, no diagnosis you're not qualified to make — and a plan with her name in it as well as yours. Parents can't fix what they haven't clearly heard; children can't be helped by adults who spent the ten minutes protecting each other's feelings.</p>

        <p>One more thing, and it governs everything: the parent is not the audience for this news. She's the other half of the team that acts on it.</p>

        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Think of a time someone gave you mixed feedback badly — all cushion, or all blade. What did you actually walk away knowing?</li>
            <li>Why do parents hear criticism of a child as criticism of themselves? Can a sentence be built to prevent that?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="whos">
            <div class="who"><b>You</b><span>Lena's English teacher. Has the exercise book with the missing pages, the essay from October that proves the talent, and ten minutes.</span></div>
            <div class="who"><b>Mrs Novak</b><span>Straight from a shift, third teacher tonight. Loves her daughter fiercely, braced for judgement, and quicker to defend than to listen — until she's sure you're on Lena's side.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip"><b>First:</b> read the conversation and watch the order of operations: warmth → real praise with evidence → the problem, named specifically → the parent invited in as the expert → the two-sided plan → the checkpoint. Then notice the sentence that saves the whole meeting when it wobbles.</div>""",

