"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for delivering mixed news about someone to someone who loves them — parents' evenings, appraisals, care meetings, references. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> The parent across the table is listening for one thing under all your words: <i>is this person for my child or against her?</i> Every phrase here answers "for" — which is precisely what earns you the right to be completely honest about the problem.</div>

        <h3>🗺️ Framing the meeting</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the map of the whole conversation, given upfront. Complete it:<br><br>"There's a lot going well, and one thing I want us to work on ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"There's a lot going well, and one thing I want us to work on together."</b><br>The honest map, drawn before the journey: proportion ("a lot" versus "one thing") and partnership ("us… together") in a single sentence. Now she knows the shape of the next ten minutes — no ambush coming, no whitewash either. And "work on" beats every synonym for "problem": it arrives already pointed at a solution.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "There's a lot going well, and one thing I want us to work on together." Appraisals, project reviews, difficult references — the mixed-news opener.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> making the praise believable — not packaging. Which version?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "She's a lovely girl, really lovely, we're all very fond of her. Now—"</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "I'll start with the good, because it's real."</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "Before I get to the problems, the positives."</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>B — "I'll start with the good, because it's real."</b><br>Every parent knows the feedback sandwich, and A tastes exactly like its bread — vague fondness, no evidence, clearly the run-up to something. C openly labels the praise as an appetiser. B does something disarming: it names the suspicion ("you think this is the polite bit") and denies it — then must immediately pay for that claim with specifics. The essay. The Amir story. Praise with evidence is the only kind that survives the meeting.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'll start with the good, because it's real." Then prove it, with named examples, before any "but" arrives.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> naming the problem — as a pattern, with numbers. Complete it:<br><br>"What I'm seeing is homework not coming in — most ___, not just ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"What I'm seeing is homework not coming in — most weeks, not just once."</b><br>Three disciplines in one sentence. "What I'm seeing" claims only your own evidence — observation, not verdict. The pattern-not-incident framing ("most weeks, not just once") is what makes it serious without exaggeration. And the numbers — four of the last six, sharply since half term — do the convincing that adjectives never could. Vague concerns get dismissed; documented patterns get acted on.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "What I'm seeing is ___ — most weeks, not just once." Feedback of every kind: describe the pattern, date the change, bring the count.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🛡️ Protecting the child from the problem</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> separating what she can't do from what she isn't doing. Complete it:<br><br>"This isn't about ___ — it's about something getting in the way."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"This isn't about ability — it's about something getting in the way."</b><br>The sentence that decides whether the parent leaves frightened or mobilised. "She can't" is a verdict about the child; "something's in the way" is a puzzle the adults can solve — and the October essay sitting on the table is the proof the distinction is real. Notice also what the teacher refuses to do: name the something. No amateur diagnosis, no guessing at bedrooms. The evidence stops where the classroom door does.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "This isn't about ability — it's about ___ getting in the way." Struggling colleagues, failing students, your own bad month.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> handing the parent the expert's chair. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>does any of this</span><span>You know her</span><span>ring true at home?</span><span>better than I do —</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"You know her better than I do — does any of this ring true at home?"</b><br>The hinge of the meeting. Until this sentence the parent is a defendant hearing charges; after it she's a consultant being briefed — and consultants contribute instead of contesting. <b>Ring true</b> = match what you already know. It's also simply honest: you have four hours a week of the child; she has twelve years. The quietness-since-half-term detail only surfaced because this question invited it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "You know ___ better than I do — does any of this ring true?" Parents, partners of patients, managers of your colleagues — recruit the person with the fuller picture.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> sizing the problem honestly — serious, and solvable. Complete it:<br><br>"It's nothing we can't ___ ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"It's nothing we can't turn around."</b><br>Said <i>after</i> the problem has been named in full — that's what separates it from a platitude. The reasons come attached: she's twelve, the talent is proven, it's been weeks not years. Hope with evidence is fuel; hope without it is anaesthetic. And "we" is still doing quiet work — whoever's turning this around, it's plural.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "It's nothing we can't turn around" — but only after the honest accounting, never instead of it.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>📋 The plan, and the exit</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the two-sided plan — your half first. Complete it:<br><br>"Here's what I'll do at school — and here's where you can ___ at ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Here's what I'll do at school — and here's where you can help at home."</b><br>Order is everything: your commitments first (Friday check-ins, the shared log), hers second — and hers framed as "help", not homework-for-parents. Note how concrete both halves are: not "more support" but a named day, a visible log, an essay on a kitchen table. A plan the parent can picture is a plan that survives the drive home.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Here's what I'll do — and here's where you can help." Every joint problem, at work and at home: your half first, their half specific.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the checkpoint — so tonight isn't a one-off. Complete it:<br><br>"Let's speak again in three ___ and see where we ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Let's speak again in three weeks and see where we are."</b><br>A plan without a checkpoint is a wish. The date turns tonight's alignment into a project with a review built in — and "I'll ring you, you won't have to chase me" removes the burden from the person already carrying most of it. Three weeks is deliberate: long enough for the plan to bite, short enough that drift can't set in.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Let's speak again in ___ and see where we are." Close every hard conversation with the date of the next one.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the sentence she should carry home. Complete it:<br><br>"She's a good kid having a hard ___ — those are ___ things."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"She's a good kid having a hard patch — those are different things."</b><br><b>A hard patch</b> — a difficult period that passes. This is the meeting's parting gift, and its deepest idea: separating who someone <i>is</i> from what they're <i>going through</i>. A parent who drives home holding "good kid, hard patch" treats the child as someone to help; one holding "she's been lying to me" treats her as someone to confront. Same facts — entirely different Tuesday at that kitchen table. Say it last; last sentences are the ones that get kept.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "___ is a good ___ having a hard patch — those are different things." Colleagues, friends, yourself in a bad season.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the opening that sets the whole temperature. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="She sits down, braced, coat still on. Your first words…"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "Thanks for coming in — I've been looking forward to this one."</b><br>Seven words against ten years of parents'-evening dread. "Looking forward to this one" only lands because it's checkably true — you were, because of the essay, because of Amir — and it instantly reclassifies the meeting from tribunal to conversation between two people on the same side. The coat came off her shoulders in the last line of the scene; it started coming off here.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Thanks for coming in — I've been looking forward to this one." Any meeting the other person has been dreading more than you have.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten work wherever you must tell someone hard truths about someone they love — including appraisals, where the person they love is themselves. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"There's a lot going well, and one thing I want us to work on together." — think of real mixed news you owe someone. What's the "lot", and what's the "one thing"?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The person, the good, the one thing…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"What I'm seeing is ___ — most weeks, not just once." — turn a vague concern you hold into a dated, counted pattern.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The pattern, with numbers…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"A good kid having a hard patch." — who in your life needs this reclassification right now?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The person, and what changes if you believe it…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="english_at_work_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every English at Work lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> the meeting, out loud, in order — looking forward to this one → a lot going well, one thing together → the good, because it's real → what I'm seeing, most weeks → not ability, something in the way → you know her better, ring true? → nothing we can't turn around → my half, your half → three weeks → good kid, hard patch. Ten minutes, both chairs still friends.</div>""",

"""        <h2>Your Turn <span class="em">🎭</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Now the little chairs are yours. Two rounds — the teacher's side, then the parent's.</p>

        <div class="timer">
            <div class="t-face" id="tface">02:00</div>
            <button class="t-btn" onclick="startTimer(120)">▶ Two minutes</button>
            <button class="t-btn" onclick="startTimer(180)">▶ Three minutes</button>
            <button class="t-btn" onclick="resetTimer()">↺ Reset</button>
        </div>

        <h3>Round 1 — you're the teacher</h3>
        <p>Invent your student, or borrow a real person you must give mixed news about — a pupil, a trainee, a team member. Know your evidence before you start: two real strengths with examples, one problem with numbers. Your teacher plays the parent — loving, braced, quick to defend.</p>

        <p class="cue">She sits down braced. Open warm, then give the map.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Thanks for coming in… There's a lot going well, and…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">The good — with evidence that proves it's not packaging.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="I'll start with the good, because it's real…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">The problem — pattern, numbers, dates. Then protect the child from it.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="What I'm seeing is… This isn't about ability…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">She bristles — "so she's lying to me?" Decline the word, and hand her the expert's chair.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="I don't think that's the most useful word… You know her better than I do…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">The plan — your half, her half, the checkpoint — and the sentence she takes home.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Here's what I'll do… Let's speak again in… She's a good kid…"></textarea>

        <h3>Round 2 — now you're the parent</h3>
        <p>Swap. Your teacher delivers mixed news about YOUR twelve-year-old. Feel it from the parent's chair: the bracing, the urge to defend, the relief when praise turns out to be real. Push back at least once — and notice which of their sentences makes you stop pushing.</p>

        <p class="cue">The problem lands. Your first, defensive response — make it honest.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="What you say when it stings…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">They ask what you're seeing at home. Decide what you share — and what you hold back.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="What rings true, what you're not ready to say…"></textarea>

        <div class="twist">
            <b>⚡ The twist</b>
            <p>Round 1 again — and when you ask whether any of this rings true at home, Mrs Novak looks at the table for a long moment and says, very quietly: "Her father moved out in March. She made me promise not to tell the school. She said she didn't want to be 'the divorce kid'. So now you know — and now I've broken a promise to her."</p>
            <p>There it is: the something in the way, and a mother holding two kinds of guilt at once. You are her English teacher, not her counsellor — but the next thirty seconds are yours. What do you say about the promise, what do you offer, and where — carefully — does this information go?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Your thirty seconds — the mother, the promise, the next step…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="check">
            <b>Before you close the tab…</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Can you draw the map — proportion and partnership — in your first minute?</li>
                <li>Does your praise carry evidence that survives suspicion?</li>
                <li>Can you name a problem as a counted pattern, not a character verdict?</li>
                <li>Can you hand the defensive person the expert's chair?</li>
                <li>Does the meeting end with two halves of a plan and a date?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>""",

"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>Marie Curie won two Nobel Prizes, but one of her strangest experiments ran at her kitchen table. Unimpressed by Parisian schools, she and a circle of professor friends built a teaching cooperative: each parent-scientist taught the children their own subject — Marie took physics, real physics, with real equipment, for ten-year-olds. She believed children could handle far more truth than schools dared give them.</p>
            <p>Her daughter Irène was a quiet, awkward girl who lost her father at eight and grew up in the shadow of the most famous woman alive — a hard patch by any measure. Marie's response was neither pressure nor protection. It was evidence-based belief: she saw the ability, named it, worked alongside it, and waited. Irène grew up to win the Nobel Prize in Chemistry herself, in 1935 — the only mother-daughter pair in the prize's history.</p>
            <p>Every parents' evening runs the same quiet experiment: an adult who has seen what a child can do, telling the people who love her — with evidence — that the ability is real and the hard patch is not the whole story. Sometimes the results take twenty years to publish.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_marie_curie.html">🔬 <span>Want the full story? The radium, the prizes, and the kitchen-table school…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Marie Curie taught children "more truth than schools dared give them." How much truth can a twelve-year-old handle — and who should decide?</li>
            <li>Who saw ability in you before you could see it yourself — and what exactly did they do about it?</li>
        </ol>""",
